title A census pays for its forks, not for its tree
prompt the baton, DIFFUSER seat -- moonshots and research; the card left me the census question
rota lap 4281, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; its test is to pull one part and see what moves, and applying it to my own elder paper's word "unclaimable" is what opened this lap -- the word is a fact about mapping and says nothing about cost
think Cold roster first, held still while it ran: 188 guards, 1630s, 183 green, 2 red, 3 gated.
think The two reds were one -- standing_equipment reds because reds_citation did (red_self). The true fault was construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md:520 claiming %614 while linking rows-613; the shelf's own header reads %613. A transcription slip in PHEROMONE's fold, repaired to %613.
think Second false claim, from my own card: standing_equipment_run.sh said present-tense that the fusion build's cheaper pass can never be earned, while standing-equipment-receipt.kyri stood written 20260908.052552 at scope full, guards 186, gated 3. Rewrote the comment to name the gated/red split 140 lines above it that lifted the constraint.
think Then the census question. I distrusted my own elder word "unclaimable" and measured instead of reasoning: eight DISCOVERY scans timed directly, dated_path at 63,107ms holding 73% of the family's 86,468ms.
think Bisected dated_path with timestamp probes at eight statement boundaries in a /tmp copy. Lines 274-283 held 68,370ms of 74,838ms, 91.4%. Its actual tree reads are git ls-files at 11ms and one grep making 29,440 pairs at 267ms -- 0.36% of the run.
think My first guess inside the bisect was wrong: I supposed the 211-basename filter loop was hot, measured 4,036ms, kept looking. The remaining ~64,300ms sat in dp_discovered_fixture_basenames, whose first statement forks one basename process per tracked file -- 16,447 of them.
think Replaced with one sed 's|.*/||'. The two agree on every path git ls-files can print, since it emits no trailing slash and no bare slash.
obs Proven twice rather than once. The stage alone reads 61,714ms to 64ms with 8,629 sprigs byte-identical by diff. The whole guard, A/B on ONE tree state by stashing the change and restoring it, reads 75,153ms to 15,070ms with all twenty output lines byte-identical.
obs The A/B mattered. An earlier reading of mine had the census at refs_lost=856, and it was an artifact of my having SOURCED the scan rather than executed it -- dirname "$0" resolves differently under sourcing. Had I trusted it I would have reported a reading change my repair never made.
obs The lantern was already lit twice in this family: empty_document_scan.sh and exec_bit_scan.sh each carry a comment naming this exact fork-per-file shape as a fault they had paid for. Neither reached the third site, because a comment repairs the file it sits in.
obs So I swept for the shape rather than assuming it was gone -- three sites tools-wide, one repaired here, one at a handful of interfaces, one over a few-hundred-entry glob. The lever is spent, and saying so beats implying more wins wait.
obs dated_path reds at HEAD and it is not mine -- proven by injecting only the reading-identical fast function into an otherwise clean tree and reading the same 100. refs_lost=100 vs LOST_CEILING=85 set 20260907.104201 with zero slack; 95 testimony, 5 living, lost_promised_living=0.
obs That gate has no closing move: the number rises whenever a ship writes a log naming a path and falls for nobody, since accrete-never-break forbids repairing testimony. The scan's own comment already proposes moving the gate to lost_promised_living and defers it to Keaton, rightly -- a gate a lap moves for itself is a ceiling raised by a side door. Surfaced, never routed around.
obs Its tier is cadence, which is why no lap-tier pass heard the breach. The repair now makes tier lap affordable at 15s, and I did not take that either -- it wants the gate question settled first.
